ction. Quality, latency and cost per query all get explicit targets."]],"related":["/ontology-knowledge-graph/","/fractional-cto/","/healthcare-data-architecture/"]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<p>You have seen the demos. Perhaps your team built one. It answered the easy questions well and then, in front of a real user, confidently said something wrong. Now legal is nervous, finance wants to know the cost per query and nobody can say whether the next release is better or worse than the last.</p>
<p>I work with leadership teams and engineering leads who are in that position, or who want to avoid it. Typical starting points:</p>
<ul>
<li>An LLM pilot that never made it past the steering committee.</li>
<li>A knowledge assistant over internal documents that users stopped trusting.</li>
<li>A plan to add agents to a workflow, with no clear view of risk or failure modes.</li>
<li>Sensitive data that cannot be sent to a public API, and a mandate to use AI anyway.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>The problems I solve</h2>
<h3>Ungrounded retrieval</h3>
<p>Chunking documents into a vector store is a starting point, not an architecture. I combine vector search with structured data and, where relationships matter, a knowledge graph (GraphRAG), so answers cite their sources and respect permissions. The <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology and knowledge graph</a> page explains why the semantic layer matters so much.</p>
<h3>No way to tell good from bad</h3>
<p>Without evaluations, every change is a guess. I build an evaluation harness from real questions early, and gate releases on it. My <a href="/insights/rag-to-production-checklist/">RAG to production checklist</a> sets out what I check before anything goes live.</p>
<h3>Agents without guardrails</h3>
<p>Multi-step agents are powerful and easy to get wrong. I use LangGraph to make state and control flow explicit, expose tools through the Model Context Protocol, and put human checkpoints where a wrong action would be expensive.</p>
<h3>Data that cannot leave the building</h3>
<p>Private, on-prem inference is now practical. I design AI gateways that classify requests by sensitivity, send regulated data to local open-weight models and route the rest to hosted APIs. More on this in <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">running private LLMs on-prem</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>My approach</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start from the decision.</strong> What will the system help someone decide or do, and what does a wrong answer cost? That sets the quality bar.</li>
<li><strong>Model the domain.</strong> Capture the key entities and relationships so retrieval and agents work from the same meaning as the business.</li>
<li><strong>Build the evaluation set before the features.</strong> Real questions, expected answers and edge cases, versioned with the code.</li>
<li><strong>Ship one thin workflow to production.</strong> With tracing, cost tracking and guardrails, behind a feature flag.</li>
<li><strong>Expand on evidence.</strong> Add sources, tools and agents only when evaluations show they help.</li>
<li><strong>Hand over the platform.</strong> Your team owns the pipelines, prompts, evaluations and runbooks.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What you get</h2>
<ul>
<li>An AI use-case portfolio ranked by value, risk and feasibility.</li>
<li>A reference architecture covering retrieval, orchestration, model routing, security and observability.</li>
<li>A working production slice: RAG or GraphRAG, LangGraph agents and MCP tools as needed.</li>
<li>An evaluation harness with quality, latency and cost targets.</li>
<li>An AI gateway pattern for authentication, PII redaction, logging and spend control.</li>
<li>Clear guidance on which workloads run on local models and which use hosted APIs.</li>
</ul>
</section>
