<section class="block">
<h2>Common mistakes I see</h2>
<h3>Choosing the model before the problem</h3>
<p>Teams often spend weeks comparing models and almost no time defining what a correct answer looks like. The model choice matters far less than the retrieval, the data and the evaluation set. With those in place, swapping models is a configuration change.</p>
<h3>Treating prompts as the product</h3>
<p>A clever prompt is easy to copy and hard to maintain. The durable value sits in your data, your domain model and the workflow around the AI: who reviews what, which actions need approval, how errors are caught.</p>
<h3>Ignoring cost until the invoice arrives</h3>
<p>Token costs scale with usage, context length and retries. I set a cost budget per query from the start, cache what can be cached, and route simple requests to smaller or local models. A system that is accurate but unaffordable at scale is not finished.</p>
<h3>Letting agents act without limits</h3>
<p>An agent that can send email, change records or call payment APIs needs the same controls as a new employee: narrow permissions, logging and approval for anything irreversible. I design those limits into the graph rather than hoping the prompt holds.</p>
<h3>No owner for quality</h3>
<p>Someone has to own the evaluation set, review failures each week and decide when a release is good enough. Without that role, quality drifts quietly. I help you define it and train the person who will hold it.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Relevant experience</h2>
<ul>
<li>Production RAG pipelines built with LangChain and LangGraph. My open-source <strong>langchain-rag-production</strong> repository shows some of the patterns.</li>
<li>Founder and builder of <strong>Wisp</strong>, a self-hostable AI platform where agents drive deployments over MCP on Kubernetes.</li>
<li>Hands-on experiments with local LLM inference on an <strong>NVIDIA DGX Spark</strong>: open-weight models, LoRA fine-tuning and quantisation, compared against hosted APIs for quality, latency and cost.</li>
<li>Co-author of peer-reviewed research in Science Immunology, which shapes how I think about evaluation: baselines, honest metrics, reproducibility.</li>
<li>Data platforms behind the AI: event-driven systems at more than a million transactions a day.</li>
</ul>
<p>For regulated settings, see <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data architecture</a>. If you need someone to own the AI agenda at leadership level, that is the <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> role. You can also browse <a href="/#work">case studies</a>, including private on-prem LLMs.</p>
</section>
